1 NOTE Names and origin: Buenos Aires records call him 'Bernardo Artayeta' (also 'Artalleta'). His 1791 marriage calls him 'natural de Navarra'. Richieri (Genealogía 16, 1976) calls him 'Simón Bernardo D'Art
2 CONC ayet, nat. de Hasparren, vasco francés, del Reino de Navarra'; Genealogia Riograndense (via Koren de Lima) wrongly calls him 'natural de Paris'. Artayet/Dartayet is an old house-derived surname in Has
2 CONC parren: Gen&O indexes Artayet baptisms there from 1630. Trade: a saddler ('talabartero', 'maestro'), later described as a 'conocido industrial' with a tanning establishment. He supplied cartridge pouc
2 CONC hes ('cartucheras') to the Real Hacienda. Events: in Aug 1806, as a volunteer at the Reconquista, he helped Liniers' wounded aide Juan B. Fantin. He testified in the Cabildo's Sept 1806 inquiry into t
2 CONC he capitulation, reporting Beresford's words in French. In Feb 1808 he was questioned by the Audiencia (oidor Bazo y Berri) with Romero, Gutiérrez, Corpet and Coll. In May 1810 he freed his slave José
2 CONC . In 1815 he gave up to the 'Patria' a 233-peso credit against the State for equipment supplied for the city's defence on 16 Apr 1815. He testified in the Brown-White incident, living near La Merced (
2 CONC Richieri: his house was half a block from La Merced, calle Cangallo between Reconquista and San Martín). His wife Estefanía Centurión died 13 Jun 1840 (per Genealogía Familiar). His own death date is
2 CONC  still unknown. (research 2026-11-21, probable)
