1 NOTE Added by research on 2026-11-20 (target I500249), confidence possible. Same person: Catarina Coronel, wife of Francisco Taveira of Vila Real and mother of Pedro Taveira de Mesquita and Mécia Taveira (
2 CONC m. 16 Feb 1572 at S. Dinis, Vila Real), is named in all three compilations (Mitoblogos citing Teixeira's 'Fidalgos e Morgados de Vila Real', the Sepúlveda blog, GenoPro). All three make her the daught
2 CONC er of Dr. Nicolau Coronel and Joana de Leão. They are not independent: all derive from the Vila Real nobiliário tradition. Nicolau himself is real and well placed. The Torre do Tombo has his 1499 fida
2 CONC lguia privilege as 'mestre Nicolau Coronel, físico', and the 1624 Apologia (Mendes dos Remédios) makes him a converted Jew and brother of D. Pedro de Castro, protonotário and benefactor of Vila Real (
2 CONC fl. 1520s-1538). That explains why a Coronel daughter married into a Vila Real family. Chronology is the problem. Nicolau was adult before 1497 and had sons by 1499, so he was born before about 1475.
2 CONC  Catarina's children were born c.1550-1575 (Mécia married 1572; GenoPro estimates 1556, 1569, 1573), which puts her birth at about 1520-1535, when Nicolau would have been 50-70. That is possible with a
2 CONC  young second wife (FamilySearch makes Joana b. 1510), but nobiliários often drop a generation, and she could equally be his granddaughter, e.g. a daughter of one of the sons named in 1499. No record
2 CONC  names her parents. Hence possible, not probable. GenoPro gives her mother as Joana de Leão; Maria de Castro is the paternal grandmother.
1 BIRT
2 DATE BEF 1475
2 PLAC Segóvia, Castela, Espanha
1 NOTE Mestre / Dr. Nicolau Coronel, físico (physician). The Torre do Tombo has a privilege of fidalguia dated 6 MAY 1499 to 'mestre Nicolau Coronel, físico em especial, e a seus filhos' (Chancelaria de D. M
2 CONC anuel I, liv. 16, fl. 108v, per the catalogue; the 1928 article says it is also copied in 'livro 4º dos Místicos', fl. 116). The grant was for service as physician to the queens D. Isabel (d. 1498) an
2 CONC d D. Maria. The 1624 'Apologia', quoted by Mendes dos Remédios (1895), names 'mestre Niculao', later physician to Queen Isabel, and his brother D. Pedro de Castro, protonotário apostólico in Vila Real
2 CONC , as men of the Jewish 'nação' who had already become Christians and who preached to the Jews held in the Estaus in 1497. So he was a converso and was adult before 1497. The tradition that he was born
2 CONC  in Segovia and studied medicine at Salamanca comes from nobiliário sources (Teixeira via Mitoblogos; Sepúlveda blog). FamilySearch KH8Y-LY9 gives 1475–1524 without sources. A birth in 1475 barely fit
2 CONC s a man who was the queen's physician and had sons by 1499, and the death year 1524 is unsupported. GenoPro I93928.
