1 NOTE Nova Friburgo affair, 1819-1820: he came back to Rio on the Camillus on 8 Feb 1820 with the last colonists (H. Bon). The colonists and the Brazilian authorities blamed him for the overcrowded ships, t
2 CONC he long wait in Holland, the deaths (more than 300 on board) and for charging the emigrants for a passage the Crown was paying; the inspector Monsenhor Miranda and D. João VI refused to receive him, h
2 CONC is canton withdrew his diplomatic credentials, and he was never allowed to settle at Nova Friburgo. He had bought the Fazenda dos Inhames on the edge of the colony before the colonists came and kept a
2 CONC  house in the town; on 27 Apr 1820 his employee Claude Clerc asked for both to be seized for a loan of 100$000 and two and a half years' wages, saying Gachet was 'perhaps about to flee'. Early in 1820
2 CONC  thirty-four colonists claimed 9,012.10 French francs from him. Monsenhor Miranda's 'Reflexoens ... sobre a violação e omissoens de Gachet' (1820) and the printed 'Obrigação' on the transport contract
2 CONC  are in the Biblioteca Nacional. The Treasury report of 1823 still left open 'what Gachet shows to be owed to him'. In Switzerland a court acquitted him and he published a defence (Reichlen). The HLS
2 CONC  dates his consulship from 1819 and Dodis ends it in 1823; no source read in this round gives the act that ended it. (research 2026-11-21, probable)
