1 NOTE Burgher of Gruyères (canton of Fribourg), son of 'feu le lieutenant Gachet' (dead before Nov 1791). He lived in Paris, parish of Saint-Sulpice: his wife was born there in 1746 and their son Sébastien-
2 CONC Nicolas was born on 27 Oct 1770 and baptised at Saint-Sulpice. A deed drawn up at Gruyères on 2 Nov 1791 says he still lived in Paris, without giving his occupation. Reichlen (1897) says the family ha
2 CONC d moved to France at the beginning of the 18th century and that he was a guard in the Cent-Suisses, the Swiss foot company of the King's household; Nicoulin (1973) found nothing to prove it and asks w
2 CONC hether he was a 'Suisse de porte' (house porter) or a Swiss guard. He was back in Gruyères by early 1798: the commune elected 'M. Joseph-Emmanuel-Eloi Gachet, président' of its provisional committee o
2 CONC f surveillance; on 13 Feb 1798 he sat at the assembly of Payerne as deputy of the burghers of Gruyères and was one of the five deputies sent to Fribourg to demand that the town join the new canton; in
2 CONC  1798-1799 he was president of the district court (tribunal de district) and bursar of the hospital of Gruyères. Probably born about 1735-1748 (his wife was born in 1746). Birth, marriage (before 1770
2 CONC , probably in Paris) and death not found. (research 2026-11-23, probable)
